  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "For a section 44 examination objection, similarity to a business name alone is not enough: the statutory comparison is with another registered trade mark or a trade mark for which registration is being sought.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(a) the applicant’s trade mark is substantially identical with, or deceptively similar to: (i) a trade mark registered by another person in respect of similar goods or closely related services; or (ii) a trade mark whose registration in respect of similar goods or closely related services is being sought by another person;"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part19A/2/2/3",
          "quote": "Registration of a business name in itself creates no intellectual property rights and is not prima facie evidence that a sign has been used ‘as a trade mark’."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "both"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual recognises that the same name can function both as a business name and as a trade mark, so its status as a business name does not itself exclude trade mark relevance.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part19A/2/2/3",
          "quote": "In many cases, particularly in relation to provision of services, the trade mark and business name will be one and the same, in that the same word or phrase will serve the dual purpose of being the name under which the business is carried on, and the name which distinguishes the services of that business."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual’s section 44 practice, the enquiry concerns the impressions created by the trade marks as a whole, rather than merely the existence of a shared name or element.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1",
          "quote": "In particular, in evaluating the likelihood of confusion, the marks must be judged as a whole:"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1",
          "quote": "What needs to be assessed is the effect of the similarities and differences on the impression or recollection an ordinary consumer of the goods or services would have of the trade marks and whether this creates a real and tangible danger of confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Section 44 also requires the specified relationship between the goods or services and the relevant priority dates; resemblance between names is not the complete statutory test.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(i) a trade mark registered by another person in respect of similar goods or closely related services; or (ii) a trade mark whose registration in respect of similar goods or closely related services is being sought by another person; and (b) the priority date for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark in respect of the applicant’s goods is not earlier than the priority date for the registration of the other trade mark in respect of the similar goods or closely related services."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(i) a trade mark registered by another person in respect of similar services or closely related goods; or (ii) a trade mark whose registration in respect of similar services or closely related goods is being sought by another person; and (b) the priority date for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark in respect of the applicant’s services is not earlier than the priority date for the registration of the other trade mark in respect of the similar services or closely related goods."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The rejection requirements in section 44(1) and (2) are expressly subject to subsections (3) and (4), concerning honest concurrent use or other circumstances, and continuous prior use respectively.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "Subject to subsections (3) and (4)"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(3) If the Registrar in either case is satisfied: (a) that there has been honest concurrent use of the 2 trade marks; or (b) that, because of other circumstances, it is proper to do so; the Registrar may accept the application for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark subject to any conditions or limitations that the Registrar thinks fit to impose."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s44",
          "quote": "(4) If the Registrar in either case is satisfied that the applicant, or the applicant and the predecessor in title of the applicant, have continuously used the applicant’s trade mark for a period: (a) beginning before the priority date for the registration of the other trade mark in respect of: (i) the similar goods or closely related services; or (ii) the similar services or closely related goods; and (b) ending on the priority date for the registration of the applicant’s trade mark; the Registrar may not reject the application because of the existence of the other trade mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "These passages support guidance about section 44, but do not establish whether a business-name concern could support a different examination ground; they are not a complete account of all possible grounds.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
